From: clasy...@gmail.com <Unknown>
Date: Thursday, November 16, 2017 at 2:38:54 PM UTC-6
Subject: PEPS User Guide Fragment - Call for Icon Editing Help
To: pepswin <Unknown>


Attached to this post is a tentative copy of Figure 3 of the 
soon-to-be-released PEPS User Guide.


I finally have some "light at the end of the tunnel" in terms of the 
overall content of the manual [the actual coding is pretty much done other 
than for minor cleanup] and this is a first-look at the release version of 
the main Command window.  Most of the functions are initiated from this 
window [or from one linked to witihin the Command Window].


As with just about any aspect of Windows, how you place the controls on the 
desktop is purely user preference.  However, many will want to use 
something like this; consider it the releases' "serving suggestion". 

This example was created in Windows Vista using medium icons with 
Auto-arrange disabled and Align to grid enabled.  This is a standard 
feature only in Windows XP and Windows Vista; however, there are simple 
registry patches for Windows 7, 8 and 10 [documentation included]. [In 
these more "modern" Windows releases, these two options are permanently 
coupled together which would prevent the organization used here; the patch 
restores the original features.]


Call for Icon Editing Help.


I have no artistic skills in the slightest [in the conventional sense].  To 
me what is there are placeholders; the various colors represent a level of 
organization in and of themselves that makes it mindless to press on the 
proper icon when needed, etc.  I can apply color changes to icons that are 
mostly a single color though.


I am asking for someone with an artistic "eye" and also the ability to 
design appropriate .ICO files to replace these.


All icons will be released as public domain, although I can give credit 
within the documentation where due [if desired].  All of the current icons 
are of freeware packages anyone can download, etc. [Your work will only be 
used here].


This will be greatly appreciated, so please can someone step up to the 
"plate" on this.


Other than artwork particulars, any comments or questions welcome.

cjl

